    },
    "message": "feat(admin): make the limits editable, and stop Save deleting what it cannot see\n\nTwo problems, one of which was eating configuration.\n\nSaving anything in the admin Configuration page rewrote ~/.fcc/.env from the\nmanifest, so every key the manifest did not know about was silently deleted --\nincluding hand-written operational settings. A raised REQUEST_LOG_MAX_ROWS\nreverted to the default on the next unrelated save, which is why a retention cap\nthat had been raised kept behaving like the default one.\n\nSaving now writes back any key Settings still reads, under its own heading. A\nkey that configures nothing is still dropped: a retired provider option should\nretire rather than be preserved forever.\n\nThe other problem was that the interesting limits were module constants, so\ntuning them meant editing installed source. Promoted to settings and plumbed to\nthe code that uses them:\n\n  PROVIDER_RETRY_ATTEMPTS            retries before the chain moves on\n  STREAM_EARLY_RETRY_ATTEMPTS        retries inside one provider\n  STREAM_MIDSTREAM_RECOVERY_ATTEMPTS re-asks after a stream dies mid-answer\n  STREAM_COMMIT_HOLDBACK_SECONDS     width of the invisible-recovery window\n  RATE_LIMIT_COOLDOWN_SECONDS        pause when a 429 carries no Retry-After\n  CREDENTIAL_CIRCUIT_THRESHOLD       failures before one key is benched\n  REQUEST_LOG_TEXT_MAX_CHARS         longest stored text, so also search reach\n  REQUEST_LOG_COMPRESSION_LEVEL      zstd level for stored bodies\n  REQUEST_LOG_QUEUE_MAX_SIZE         pending writes before records are dropped\n\nA holdback of 0 now commits the first event immediately instead of buffering it\nuntil some later push happens to check the clock.\n\nAll of these, plus the fallback deadlines and the request-log retention\nsettings, appear in a new \"Limits\" section of the admin config: 18 fields whose\ndefaults are asserted to match the code's own defaults, so the form cannot show\na number the server does not use.\n\nMINOR: new config options and admin fields, backward compatible.",
